ADS-018 — Data Governance

Document ID: ADS-018
Title: Data Governance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Data Governance Model (CDGM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Data Governance Model establishes the principles, responsibilities, controls, and policies required to govern Canonical Atlas Objects throughout their lifecycle while preserving engineering integrity, trust, compliance, and interoperability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Governance architecture
Governance principles
Roles and responsibilities
Ownership
Stewardship
Data quality governance
Security governance
Change governance
Audit governance

This specification applies to every Canonical Atlas Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Data Governance is the disciplined management of Canonical Atlas Objects through defined ownership, stewardship, policies, controls, and accountability to preserve engineering integrity, trust, quality, and lifecycle continuity.

Governance manages the object.

It does not alter its engineering semantics.

4. Objectives

The Data Governance Model SHALL:

Preserve engineering integrity.
Define accountability.
Protect canonical semantics.
Ensure policy compliance.
Support auditability.
Maintain data quality.
Enable federation.
Support long-term preservation.
5. Governance Principles

Every Atlas implementation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Accountability

Every Canonical Atlas Object SHALL have an accountable owner.

Stewardship

Every governed object SHALL have an assigned steward.

Policy-Driven Governance

Governance decisions SHALL be based upon explicit policies.

Transparency

Governance activities SHALL be traceable.

Separation of Duties

Critical governance activities SHALL support role separation.

Continuous Governance

Governance SHALL remain active throughout the object lifecycle.

6. Governance Roles

Atlas defines the following governance roles.

Role	Responsibility
Owner	Business accountability
Steward	Data quality and governance
Custodian	Technical management
Producer	Creates object
Consumer	Uses object
Reviewer	Evaluates object
Approver	Grants approval
Auditor	Verifies compliance

Organizations MAY introduce additional governance roles.

7. Governance Domains

Atlas defines six governance domains.

Ownership Governance

Defines:

Ownership
Accountability
Delegation
Succession
Quality Governance

Defines:

Validation
Data quality
Quality metrics
Improvement actions
Security Governance

Defines:

Classification
Access
Authorization
Confidentiality
Lifecycle Governance

Defines:

Creation
Approval
Publication
Archival
Retirement
Change Governance

Defines:

Version approval
Change requests
Impact assessment
Release management
Compliance Governance

Defines:

Regulatory compliance
Engineering standards
Organizational policies
Audit readiness
8. Governance Policies

Every governed object SHALL reference applicable policies.

Examples include:

Security Policy
Retention Policy
Validation Policy
Access Policy
Classification Policy
Version Policy
Archival Policy
Compliance Policy

Policies SHALL be versioned Canonical Atlas Objects.

